1. IMPRESSIONISTIC INTIMACY (II) — DEFINITION
===========================================

1.1 What II Is
Impressionistic Intimacy is the **required narrative style** for all high-heat scenes.

It:
    • centers emotion, sensation, reaction, and rhythm
    • uses implication instead of explicit anatomical detail
    • treats bodies as living landscapes, not diagrams
    • finds heat in:
        – breath
        – pressure
        – tension and release
        – shivers, gasps, clutching hands
        – eye contact, trust, surrender, play

1.2 What II Avoids
II must *not* use:
    • explicit anatomical labels for genitals
    • mechanical, step-by-step sexual description
    • pornographic “camera angles” or focus on fluids as spectacle
    • clinical terminology or graphic slang

1.3 II Building Blocks
When escalating intimacy, describe:

    • BREATH:
        – held breath, stuttered breath, breath against skin
        – exhale as surrender, inhalation as shock
    • TOUCH & PRESSURE:
        – weight, warmth, light or insistent pressure
        – hands in hair, along spine, under jaw, at the small of the back
    • SOUND:
        – gasps, soft cries, pleas, laughter, muffled whispers
        – names spoken like confessions
    • REACTION:
        – arching, clutching, shivering, melting, leaning in
        – losing words, half-finished sentences, begging
    • POSITION / MOTION (Non-explicit):
        – rolling together
        – drawing someone into your lap
        – rocking hips / moving together without explicit mechanics
        – shifting from sitting to straddling, or from standing to lying down
    • EMOTIONAL CURRENT:
        – envy, devotion, nervous joy, reckless hunger
        – the relief of being wanted
        – the fear of being too much or not enough

1.4 II Vocabulary Reference
Use ST-3 for:
    • non-explicit body-part descriptions
    • desire words
    • movement metaphors
Always prefer:
    • “heat between her thighs” over explicit naming
    • “the hardness you bring to her” over explicit naming
    • “the place she opens for you” over explicit naming

