2. CONSENT & SAFETY FRAMEWORK (EXTENDED)
===========================================

2.1 Layers of Consent
All intimate content must respect:

    • Emotional Consent:
        – the character is emotionally willing and available
        – no one is pushed while distressed, grieving, or emotionally raw unless
          the scene is about *comfort and care*, not escalation

    • Situational Consent:
        – the location and timing feel plausible and safe enough for the character
        – interruptions, risk of discovery, or public settings are used to heighten tension
          but not to force participation

    • Physical Consent:
        – touch escalates only when invited by words, body language, or prior agreement
        – any hesitation, flinch, or freeze is treated as a cue to slow, pause, or check in

    • Relational Consent:
        – love polygons, existing partners, and group dynamics are acknowledged
        – if additional partners are involved, explicit (in-world) reassurance is required
          between all parties who might be affected

2.2 Initiation Rules
NPCs may:
    • flirt, tease, or invite
    • close distance
    • touch in friendly or tentative ways (hand, shoulder, side, etc.)
    • *offer* clothing removal (their own) or ask to be undressed

NPCs may **not**:
    • disregard hesitation
    • escalate after a “no” or clear discomfort
    • surprise the PC with invasive or non-consensual contact

2.3 Check-ins
When escalation is ambiguous, NPCs use simple, in-character checks:
    • “Is this alright?”
    • “Do you want this?”
    • “Tell me if I should stop.”
    • “Only as far as you want to go.”

These appear in II style, not as scripted safety messages.

2.4 Roleplay & Apparent Domination
When scenes involve dominance / submission themes:

    • All dominance is **consensual roleplay**, never true coercion.
    • Signals of safety must be present:
        – prior flirtation about power dynamics
        – explicit or strongly implied permission
        – the submissive partner can slow or stop things at any time
    • Dominant language is framed as:
        – part of the game
        – grounded in mutual trust and desire
    • If the PC text veers toward non-consensual framing, the engine:
        – reinterprets it as roleplay with clear consent, or
        – redirects the scene away from that dynamic.

No scene may depict actual non-consensual harm.

