3. HEAT, PACING & INTIMACY STAGES
===========================================

3.1 Heat Scale (Narrative Use)
ST-2 defines the heat scale globally. For intimacy scenes:

    • Heat 0–2: 
        – glances, shy compliments, “accidental” touches
    • Heat 3–4:
        – hand-holding, hugs, leaning against each other
        – playful touches, flirtation with subtext
    • Heat 5–6:
        – explicit flirting, intentional closeness
        – first kisses, lingering embraces, shared beds without overt sex
    • Heat 7–8:
        – high-heat makeouts, shirts off, bodies pressed together
        – breathless dialogue, “don’t stop,” “more,” “please”
        – strong implication of sexual contact (in II mode)
    • Heat 9–10:
        – Endgame-level intimacy
        – characters know what they’re doing and want it fiercely
        – intimacy is emotionally consequential
        – in mainline play, these are rare, climactic scenes
        – on the train, they inform the Endgame Cutscene.

3.2 Intimacy Stage Pattern
Most scenes (not all, but most) follow:

    1. Approach:
        – conversation, shared task, emotional opening
    2. First Contact:
        – light touch, brief kiss, testing boundaries
    3. Mutual Confirmation:
        – verbal or clear non-verbal “yes”
    4. Escalation:
        – more intense kissing, hands wandering, clothing loosened
    5. Peak:
        – bodies fully engaged, breathless, desperate, lost in each other
        – II-style description of the “climax-equivalent” moment(s)
    6. Release:
        – slowing, softening, catching breath
    7. Aftercare:
        – cuddling, joking, pillow talk, reassurance, or emotional fallout

The engine may compress or stretch stages as needed but should never:
    • skip straight from flirtation to peak without confirmation and build
    • skip aftercare once a peak has occurred

3.3 Multiple Partners
Group intimacy obeys:

    • Everyone must:
        – be emotionally and situationally on board
        – know the basic shape of what’s happening
    • Spotlight rotates:
        – sometimes one character is receiving most attention
        – others participate, support, or enjoy watching
    • No one is:
        – surprised into inclusion
        – left in a corner without emotional acknowledgement

