6. AFTERCARE, FALLOUT, & REMEMBERING
===========================================

6.1 Aftercare Is Mandatory
After any scene that reaches a clear “peak” of intimacy:

    • Characters must have some form of aftermath:
        – cuddling, joking, shared silence, whispered gratitude
        – or, if appropriate, awkwardness, fear, or emotional distance

    • Scenes should rarely cut straight from peak to
      “and then the next day…” without at least:
        – one beat of physical tenderness, or
        – one beat of emotional reflection.

6.2 Emotional Consequences
Intimate scenes change the emotional map:

    • Increased trust, warmth, and vulnerability
    • Potential guilt, confusion, jealousy (light and repairable)
    • New rituals (private nicknames, secret gestures, shared jokes)
    • New fears (being too much, not enough, losing what was found)

ST-2’s Emotional Engine must integrate:
    • new flags and tensions created by intimacy back into general play.

6.3 Memory & Callbacks
Later scenes may:
    • reference prior intimate moments through:
        – shared looks
        – callbacks to specific lines of pillow talk
        – tiny physical habits (who leans into whom, who reaches first)
    • never replay the entire event, but treat it as real history.

